red:** Accepting worker-published empty `ok`/`degraded` snapshots is safer for pump/API honesty than indefinitely retaining an older, larger in-memory LKG that worker already superseded on disk.\n\nExtreme numeric inputs (`NaN`, `1e308`, etc.) are not on this path; no overflow/never-expires finding.\n\n## Finding (P2) — startup vs hot reload\n\n**Measured:** `_load_initial_snapshot` (lines ~655–664 at head) still requires `isinstance(payload, dict) and payload.get(\"netuids\")` (truthy). An on-disk file with `netuids: []` and `status: \"degraded\"` is now accepted on mtime bump via `reload_from_disk_if_stale` but still falls through to `emergency_registry` on process cold start.\n\n**Inferred:** That diverges web behavior after restart vs long-lived reload — opposite failure modes (registry emergency vs honoring worker empty/degraded). Same PR intent should apply the identical acceptance predicate to `_load_initial_snapshot`.\n\n## Owner alignment\n\n**Inferred:** Writers publish; web reloads only (`is_universe_refresh_writer` / `ensure_universe_reader`) — change is in the correct function. Honoring worker disk state matches split worker/web ownership.\n\n## Test gap\n\n**Inferred:** No test files in fixture tree for this PR; recommend a unit test: persist `{netuids: [], status: \"degraded\", ...}`, bump mtime, assert `reload_from_disk_if_stale` updates snapshot (and mirrored `_load_initial_snapshot` after fix).",
